# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored -- keep it that way, a
# beamtime directory gets shared, archived and sometimes published.
#
# Precedence, highest first:
#   1. the shell environment
#   2. this file
#   3. ~/.nrw
#   4. ~/.aure   (so a machine already set up for AuRE works here unchanged)
#
# ISAAC_URL, ISAAC_KEY, AURE_OAUTH_WELL_KNOWN_URL, AURE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET,
# AURE_LDAP_URL and AURE_LDAP_BASE_DN are the exception: this file is never
# read for them (see the end).
#
# `nrw doctor` reports which of these it actually read. The Settings page of
# `nrw serve` (Language model) can choose the Claude Code CLI below for you:
# it writes LLM_PROVIDER and LLM_MODEL into `.env` and changes nothing else.
#
# Needed only by what asks a language model: `nrw model new --from-notes`,
# `nrw aure run`, the judgement in `nrw assess`, and the conditions
# `nrw isaac export` reads from sample.md -- on the pages, New model, Quick fit
# with AuRE and ISAAC's Export. Everything else in nr-workbench runs offline;
# if you have no endpoint, use `nrw model new --print-prompt` and hand that to
# the coding assistant already open on this project.

# --- one of these -----------------------------------------------------------

# The Claude Code CLI you already have. No key, no URL: AuRE runs `claude -p`
# as a subprocess and it uses whatever that binary is authenticated with --
# a subscription, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, or your Bedrock / Vertex / Foundry setup.
# Costs more per call than a plain endpoint (~12k extra input tokens of Claude
# Code preamble) and adds ~1s of process startup; see AuRE's README.
# LLM_PROVIDER=claude_code
# AURE_CLAUDE_BIN=/path/to/claude   # only if it is not on PATH

# A hosted provider
# LLM_PROVIDER=openai
# LLM_API_KEY=sk-...
# LLM_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini

# Google
# LLM_PROVIDER=gemini
# LLM_API_KEY=...
# LLM_MODEL=gemini-2.0-flash

# A local or self-hosted OpenAI-compatible endpoint (ollama, vLLM, LM Studio).
# No API key needed; the base URL is what marks it usable.
# LLM_PROVIDER=local
# LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# LLM_MODEL=llama3.1

# ALCF (Argonne). Uses a token from `alcf-auth`, not an API key.
# LLM_PROVIDER=alcf
# ALCF_CLUSTER=sophia

# --- optional ---------------------------------------------------------------

# 0 is the default and the right choice here: filling in a spec from notes is
# extraction, not invention.
# LLM_TEMPERATURE=0
# LLM_TIMEOUT=120

# --- ISAAC ------------------------------------------------------------------

# The ISAAC Portal `nrw isaac push` publishes a final fit to, and your key for
# it. NOT read from this file: put them in ~/.nrw (or your shell). Anyone who
# can write this project can write this file, and a portal named here would
# choose where your key and your records are sent.
#
# ISAAC_URL=https://isaac.slac.stanford.edu/portal/api
# ISAAC_KEY=...

# --- AuRE OAuth -------------------------------------------------------------

# The "Log in" link in `nrw serve`. Will have to adjust for your specific
# provider values.
# AURE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=aure-workbench
# Space-separated list of OAuth scopes to request.
# AURE_OAUTH_SCOPES=openid email profile user_id
#
# The provider and the client secret are NOT read from this file: put them in
# ~/.nrw (or your shell), as for ISAAC above. A provider named here would
# choose where every login is sent, and a secret here travels with the project.
#
# AURE_OAUTH_WELL_KNOWN_URL=http://localhost:8080/realms/master/.well-known/openid-configuration
# AURE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=secret

# --- LDAP -------------------------------------------------------------------

# The directory `nrw serve` looks up a logged-in user's posixGroups in. Must be
# an ldaps:// URL; the server's certificate is verified.
#
# NOT read from this file: put them in ~/.nrw (or your shell), as for the OAuth
# provider above. A directory named here would decide which groups every login
# is given.
#
# AURE_LDAP_URL=ldaps://snsldap-vip.ornl.gov
# AURE_LDAP_BASE_DN=dc=sns,dc=ornl,dc=gov

# --- Settings ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Let a user logged in to `nrw serve` (the "Log in" link above) change this
# project's settings -- the Settings page -- without the one-time link
# `nrw serve` prints, if they are a member of the project's IPTS or of the
# data_access group. Every other change still needs that link. Off unless set
# to 1, true, yes or on.
# USER_ALLOW_SETTINGS=false
